ncumbency": 2.0, "money": 0.08}, "rating_margin": 4.3, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.25, "baseline_margin": 5.53, "analyst_adjustment": -1.03, "key_factors": ["Moskowitz's incumbency and large cash advantage ($1.9M vs $476K)", "Singer's late momentum: Chamber, police and NFIB endorsements, first TV ads, and outside spending edge", "Ratings split between Tossup and Lean D; markets price the Democrat around 70%", "No recent general-election polling; May surveys showed a double-digit Moskowitz lead"], "rationale": "The fundamentals favor Moskowitz — incumbency, a big cash edge, and May polling showing a wide lead — but Singer's endorsement haul, TV launch, and outside-spending edge give Republicans a credible path in an R+3 district. Ratings lean Democratic with several Tossup calls, so a modest trim from the fundamentals-based margin fits the evidence.", "watch": "Super PAC air war and voters' views of Trump in this South Florida seat could swing the race either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Jared Moskowitz (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Jared Moskowitz, a first-term Democrat in an R+3 South Florida district, faces Republican Scott Singer, the former Boca Raton mayor. Moskowitz holds a large financial advantage, with $1.9 million on hand to Singer's $476 thousand, though Singer has benefited from roughly $900 thousand in outside support and has the airwaves to himself so far.\n\nSinger has racked up endorsements from the U.S. Chamber, police groups, and NFIB, and even a Democratic former congressman crossed party lines to back him. Expert ratings split between Tossup and Lean D, and prediction markets price the Democrat's chances around 70 percent. With no recent public polling, the race looks like a genuine but Democratic-tilted contest.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7798, "margin": 4.68}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7278, "mar